marker + push. I could spawn a verification/review sub-agent to check acceptance criteria and run tests. Then I do the marker commit/push myself (since that's marker creation, which is my job — \"you only do coordination, verification of results, and creation of completion markers\").\n\nPractically:\n1. Quickly check the current state (git status, existence of package.json, tests). I can run read-only bash checks myself — coordination/verification is allowed.\n2. Spawn a code-reviewer sub-agent to verify the story's acceptance criteria and run the test suite (review phase).\n3. Once PASS: restore the marker, verify, and commit/push.\n\nActually, wait — git status at the start of the conversation shows `D .story-updates/STORY-setup-001a.passed` (deleted, staged? \" D\" is the working-tree state). Hmm, the status line is: `D .story-updates/STORY-setup-001a.passed` — is the leading character a space? It's displayed as `D .story-updates/...` — in the list it looks like \"D .\" with a leading space, meaning deleted in the working tree but not staged.\n\nAlso untracked: .last-branch, .ralph/, .story-updates/processed/, prd.json.bak, ralph-summary.txt, ralph.log.\n\nCommitting `git add .` would include prd.json.bak, ralph.log, etc. — maybe that's what happened before? Actually, the previous commit df2c3f0 \"auto-split STORY-setup-001a into 5 sub-stories\" was probably an orchestrator commit. Hmm, but the marker is tracked at HEAD, so some commit added it.\n\nWait — according to the retry log, the marker is tracked at HEAD with 0 bytes, and was deleted from the working tree. Restoring via touch would match HEAD. Then git status would be clean with respect to that file. No new commit needed (though phase 5 instructs committing. If there's nothing to commit, the commit would be empty — I could just note that it's already committed). Then push: push HEAD df2c3f0 to origin (upstream is at 6cc5d66, one behind).\n\nFirst let me check the current state myself (read-only), then spawn the review sub-agent to verify the acceptance criteria and run the tests. Actually, per the contract I must not write code, but read-only verification commands are fine as \"verifying results.\"\n\nPlan:\n1. Bash: cd into the workspace and check the contents of package.json, git log, git status, and whether tests exist.\n2. Spawn a code-reviewer (ecc-code-reviewer.md) sub-agent to verify the acceptance criteria + run the test command. Pass the entire contents of the agent file + the task.\n3. Based on the result: if PASS → restore the marker, ls to verify, commit (if necessary), push.\n\nThe sub-agent needs the contents of the agent file — I have to read the file first. Let me read ecc-code-reviewer.md and check the current state in parallel.\n\nNote: spawning the Agent tool — must not set the model, subagent_type, run_in_background, or name. Just prompt + description. Wait with TaskOutput block:true.\n\nAlright, let's get started.\n","signature":"b20076d2dbb24fb29962b09ff91ef93c"}],"model":"unsloth/Qwen3.8-27B-NVFP4","stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":48014,"output_tokens":0},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"d806bf89-72b7-4fda-a818-7a027911e543","uuid":"24684e12-3c33-4504-8935-b04b14975155","timestamp":"2026-08-20T08:46:55.704Z"}
